

Why:

How:

Log:

Test:

Refs:
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# FIRST LINE: <type>(<scope>): <imperative summary, max 72 chars>
#   type: feat | fix | refactor | perf | test | docs | chore | build | ci
#   e.g.  fix(payment): prevent double capture on client retry
#
# Why:  1-3 lines. The problem or root cause, not the code.
# How:  The approach, plus the thinking method used.
#       Method: hypothesis | 5-whys | first-principles | trade-off | bisect | spike
# Log:  ASCII signifiers (same meaning as the colored ones in PRs):
#         x done (✅)   ~ dropped, say why (❌)   > moved (➡️)
#         ! insight (💡)   ? question (❓)   - note (📝)   o event (🔵)
#         < waiting (⏳)   . open (⬜)   !! risk (⚠️)   * priority (⭐)
# Test: How this was verified (command, scenario, or "manual: steps").
# Refs: Azure Boards work items. Keep as the last paragraph.
#         #1234          links the work item
#         Fixes #1234    also resolves it when the commit reaches the
#                        default branch (push, or PR completion without
#                        squash + "Complete associated work items")
#         Record: docs/changes/YYYY-MM-DD-<id>-<slug>.md
#       Never start a line with #1234: git treats it as a comment and
#       deletes it. Write "Refs: #1234" instead.
#
# Small commits: first line + Why + Test is enough. Delete unused labels.
# Resolution depends on repository settings; squash-message edits are not
# a guaranteed resolution workaround.
# Lines starting with '#' are removed automatically.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
